and this isn't financial advice. Always do your own research before putting real money to work. The S&amp;P 500 closed at 7,674, up 0.4%. The NASDAQ composite finished at 26,180, also up 0.4%. The TSX composite gained 0.7% to 36,620.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:01:07.460
Kevin Worsh's archived forecasts from 2007 through 2011 showed he consistently saw less productive capacity and a higher unemployment floor near 6.2% than his peers.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:24.440
Minneapolis Fed President Neil Kashkari highlighted tariff pressures, the Iran conflict, and AI build out as reasons he doubts inflation will reach target soon. The next Fed decision and Bank of Canada path remain the key macro frame for rate sensitive sectors.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:34.040
Historical Fed Governor forecasts offer a window into how central bankers privately viewed economic slack and inflation risks during periods of upheaval.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:45.420
Today's release of Kevin Worsh's quarterly submissions shows a policymaker who consistently saw less room for unemployment to fall without sparking prices than his FOMC colleagues.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:56.320
Canadian investors can apply this by cross checking current Fed projections against archived governor data to gauge whether markets are pricing in overly optimistic slack assumptions.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:08.520
Implementation starts with pulling the St. Louis Fed's Fred archive for past FOMC projections, then comparing them to today's SEP.plot using a simple spreadsheet overlay.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:19.800
The approach worked best in the 2010 through 2013 period when actual unemployment undershot the Fed's own estimates, rewarding those who positioned defensively early.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:33.740
Risk lies in overweighting one governor's view when the committee ultimately decides. Imagine you added a rate sensitive ETF to your TFSA last month after the latest.plot showed two cuts priced in.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:44.900
Your order filled cleanly but the real information edge sits in the gap between what the current committee projects and what individual governors once submitted privately during similar shocks.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:56.320
Worsh's post-crisis submissions released only after he left show he placed the sustainable unemployment floor near 6.2% while peers saw more slack.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:10.480
That single difference altered how he read inflation risks quarter after quarter. Professionals always pull the full historical distribution from Fred before the next FOMC meeting rather than relying on the headline median dot.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:19.760
Most retail investors treat the published SEP as a single consensus number and miss how wide the internal range actually was in past cycles.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:32.300
The concrete fix is to download the individual governor forecasts for the last three comparable periods and mark where today's median sits inside that historical band before sizing any duration trade.

